1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:03,802
When people picture rich people,

3
00:00:03,802 --> 00:00:05,683
they picture them buying everything.

4
00:00:05,863 --> 00:00:07,191
The yacht, the watch,

5
00:00:07,191 --> 00:00:09,216
the car with the doors that open

6
00:00:09,216 --> 00:00:09,659
upward.

7
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:12,250
But when researchers actually sat down with

8
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:15,109
American millionaires and looked at their receipts,

9
00:00:15,109 --> 00:00:16,735
they found something strange.

10
00:00:16,915 --> 00:00:19,068
Most of them were quietly refusing to

11
00:00:19,068 --> 00:00:20,872
buy the very things the rest of

12
00:00:20,872 --> 00:00:21,861
us think we need.

13
00:00:22,041 --> 00:00:24,433
Today, twenty of those things.

14
00:00:24,782 --> 00:00:26,756
Here's the number that started it all.

15
00:00:26,936 --> 00:00:29,404
In the book The Millionaire Next Door,

16
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:32,845
researchers Thomas Stanley and William Danko surveyed

17
00:00:32,845 --> 00:00:34,274
American millionaires.

18
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:36,344
Most of them said they had never

19
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:38,766
spent more than three hundred ninety nine

20
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:39,829
dollars on a suit.

21
00:00:40,179 --> 00:00:42,916
That book came out in nineteen ninety

22
00:00:42,916 --> 00:00:44,986
six, so prices have changed.

23
00:00:45,166 --> 00:00:46,524
But the pattern hasn't.

24
00:00:46,704 --> 00:00:49,958
Stanley kept studying millionaires for years after,

25
00:00:49,958 --> 00:00:52,255
and the same picture showed up again

26
00:00:52,255 --> 00:00:52,893
and again.

27
00:00:53,073 --> 00:00:55,629
The typical American millionaire is not a

28
00:00:55,629 --> 00:00:57,438
movie star or a tech founder.

29
00:00:57,618 --> 00:00:59,243
It's a business owner,

30
00:00:59,243 --> 00:01:00,943
a teacher, an engineer,

31
00:01:00,943 --> 00:01:01,681
a manager.

32
00:01:01,861 --> 00:01:04,007
Someone who earned a good income and

33
00:01:04,007 --> 00:01:05,855
simply refused to spend it all.

34
00:01:06,205 --> 00:01:08,133
And here's how this video works.

35
00:01:08,313 --> 00:01:10,527
We'll look at these twenty things from

36
00:01:10,527 --> 00:01:11,285
three angles.

37
00:01:11,465 --> 00:01:12,905
First, the numbers.

38
00:01:13,085 --> 00:01:15,650
What these purchases really cost over time.

39
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:17,804
Second, the psychology.

40
00:01:17,984 --> 00:01:19,687
Why normal people buy them,

41
00:01:19,687 --> 00:01:21,328
and why rich people don't.

42
00:01:21,508 --> 00:01:23,040
And third, the playbook.

43
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,169
What you can actually do differently,

44
00:01:25,169 --> 00:01:26,169
starting this week.

45
00:01:26,349 --> 00:01:27,050
By the end,

46
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:29,279
you'll see that being rich is often

47
00:01:29,279 --> 00:01:30,808
less about what you buy,

48
00:01:30,808 --> 00:01:32,909
and more about what you refuse to

49
00:01:32,909 --> 00:01:33,164
buy.

50
00:01:33,514 --> 00:01:35,225
Let's start with the cold,

51
00:01:35,225 --> 00:01:36,080
hard numbers.

52
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:38,745
And number one is a purchase almost

53
00:01:38,745 --> 00:01:40,235
every American makes,

54
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:41,868
usually more than once.

55
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:44,555
Every number in this video comes from

56
00:01:44,555 --> 00:01:45,503
a named source.

57
00:01:45,683 --> 00:01:49,032
Millionaire spending habits come from Thomas Stanley's

58
00:01:49,032 --> 00:01:52,071
research, including The Millionaire Next Door and

59
00:01:52,071 --> 00:01:53,125
Stop Acting Rich.

60
00:01:53,305 --> 00:01:55,584
Car prices come from Kelley Blue Book

61
00:01:55,584 --> 00:01:56,324
and Edmunds.

62
00:01:56,504 --> 00:01:58,816
Credit card rates come from the Federal

63
00:01:58,816 --> 00:01:59,290
Reserve.

64
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:02,386
Growth examples use simple stated math,

65
00:02:02,386 --> 00:02:05,600
like a seven percent average yearly return.

66
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:07,411
That's an illustration,

67
00:02:07,411 --> 00:02:08,404
not a promise.

68
00:02:08,584 --> 00:02:09,902
This is education,

69
00:02:09,902 --> 00:02:11,440
not financial advice.

70
00:02:11,790 --> 00:02:12,568
Angle one.

71
00:02:12,748 --> 00:02:13,491
The numbers.

72
00:02:13,671 --> 00:02:15,786
These are the purchases where the math

73
00:02:15,786 --> 00:02:17,845
alone is enough to make a millionaire

74
00:02:17,845 --> 00:02:18,234
say no.

75
00:02:18,584 --> 00:02:19,328
Number one.

76
00:02:19,508 --> 00:02:21,810
Rich people rarely buy a brand new

77
00:02:21,810 --> 00:02:23,165
car every few years.

78
00:02:23,515 --> 00:02:25,701
A new car starts losing value the

79
00:02:25,701 --> 00:02:27,357
moment you drive it home.

80
00:02:27,537 --> 00:02:30,462
Estimates from sources like Carfax and Edmunds

81
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:32,814
put the drop at around twenty percent

82
00:02:32,814 --> 00:02:33,959
in the first year,

83
00:02:33,959 --> 00:02:36,184
and roughly half or more after five

84
00:02:36,184 --> 00:02:38,091
years, depending on the model.

85
00:02:38,271 --> 00:02:41,022
Meanwhile, Kelley Blue Book reported that the

86
00:02:41,022 --> 00:02:43,223
average price paid for a new vehicle

87
00:02:43,223 --> 00:02:45,852
in America passed fifty thousand dollars in

88
00:02:45,852 --> 00:02:47,013
twenty twenty five.

89
00:02:47,193 --> 00:02:48,795
That's a lot of money to watch

90
00:02:48,795 --> 00:02:49,062
melt.

91
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:50,568
In Stop Acting Rich,

92
00:02:50,568 --> 00:02:53,342
Stanley found that the median price millionaires

93
00:02:53,342 --> 00:02:55,307
paid for their most recent car was

94
00:02:55,307 --> 00:02:57,271
about thirty one thousand dollars,

95
00:02:57,271 --> 00:02:58,947
in a survey from the late two

96
00:02:58,947 --> 00:02:59,525
thousands.

97
00:02:59,705 --> 00:03:00,840
Many bought used,

98
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:03,176
and many kept their cars for years.

99
00:03:03,356 --> 00:03:05,642
One of the most popular brands among

100
00:03:05,642 --> 00:03:06,658
them was Toyota,

101
00:03:06,658 --> 00:03:07,420
not Ferrari.

102
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,741
Try this: before your next car,

103
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:12,089
look up the five year resale value

104
00:03:12,089 --> 00:03:12,987
of the model.

105
00:03:13,167 --> 00:03:14,126
If it loses half,

106
00:03:14,126 --> 00:03:16,383
you're paying for that loss every month.

107
00:03:16,733 --> 00:03:19,020
Here's what that difference can grow into.

108
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,327
Say you skip a car payment upgrade

109
00:03:21,327 --> 00:03:23,831
and invest three hundred dollars a month

110
00:03:23,831 --> 00:03:24,331
instead.

111
00:03:24,511 --> 00:03:26,976
At a seven percent average yearly return,

112
00:03:26,976 --> 00:03:29,622
over twenty years that's roughly one hundred

113
00:03:29,622 --> 00:03:31,245
fifty six thousand dollars.

114
00:03:31,595 --> 00:03:32,384
Number two.

115
00:03:32,564 --> 00:03:35,072
Rich people don't pay credit card interest.

116
00:03:35,421 --> 00:03:37,437
According to the Federal Reserve,

117
00:03:37,437 --> 00:03:39,879
the average interest rate on credit card

118
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:42,689
accounts that actually paid interest was above

119
00:03:42,689 --> 00:03:44,582
twenty percent in recent years.

120
00:03:44,762 --> 00:03:46,248
Think about what that means.

121
00:03:46,428 --> 00:03:47,647
If you carry a balance,

122
00:03:47,647 --> 00:03:49,872
every hundred dollar purchase can cost you

123
00:03:49,872 --> 00:03:51,939
more than one hundred twenty dollars by

124
00:03:51,939 --> 00:03:52,999
the end of the year.

125
00:03:53,179 --> 00:03:55,453
You're paying a premium on everything you

126
00:03:55,453 --> 00:03:55,675
buy.

127
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:58,452
Many wealthy people use credit cards.

128
00:03:58,632 --> 00:04:00,891
They just pay the full balance every

129
00:04:00,891 --> 00:04:02,836
month, so the card is a payment

130
00:04:02,836 --> 00:04:03,903
tool, not a loan.

131
00:04:04,083 --> 00:04:05,655
Some collect the rewards,

132
00:04:05,655 --> 00:04:08,044
which means the card company is paying

133
00:04:08,044 --> 00:04:09,992
them, not the other way around.

134
00:04:10,172 --> 00:04:12,551
Try this: open your last statement and

135
00:04:12,551 --> 00:04:15,118
find the line that says interest charged.

136
00:04:15,298 --> 00:04:16,749
Multiply it by twelve.

137
00:04:16,929 --> 00:04:18,800
That's what your balance is costing you

138
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:19,135
a year.

139
00:04:19,485 --> 00:04:20,414
Number three.

140
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:21,628
Lottery tickets.

141
00:04:21,978 --> 00:04:24,784
Americans spend more than one hundred billion

142
00:04:24,784 --> 00:04:26,904
dollars a year on lottery tickets,

143
00:04:26,904 --> 00:04:29,773
according to the North American Association of

144
00:04:29,773 --> 00:04:31,707
State and Provincial Lotteries.

145
00:04:31,887 --> 00:04:34,315
The odds of winning the Powerball jackpot

146
00:04:34,315 --> 00:04:36,389
are about one in two hundred ninety

147
00:04:36,389 --> 00:04:37,100
two million.

148
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:39,120
You are far more likely to be

149
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:41,404
struck by lightning than to hit that

150
00:04:41,404 --> 00:04:41,912
jackpot.

151
00:04:42,261 --> 00:04:44,509
On average, lotteries pay back only a

152
00:04:44,509 --> 00:04:46,331
portion of what players spend,

153
00:04:46,331 --> 00:04:48,517
which is how states raise money from

154
00:04:48,517 --> 00:04:48,821
them.

155
00:04:49,001 --> 00:04:50,181
So, on average,

156
00:04:50,181 --> 00:04:52,147
every ticket loses money.

157
00:04:52,327 --> 00:04:53,950
Rich people do take risks,

158
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:56,072
but they take risks where the odds

159
00:04:56,072 --> 00:04:57,196
are on their side,

160
00:04:57,196 --> 00:04:59,630
like owning businesses and investments.

161
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:01,659
Try this: if you buy tickets,

162
00:05:01,659 --> 00:05:03,699
add up what you spent last year.

163
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:06,123
Then imagine that money in an index

164
00:05:06,123 --> 00:05:06,956
fund instead.

165
00:05:07,306 --> 00:05:08,212
Number four.

166
00:05:08,392 --> 00:05:11,271
Extended warranties on small electronics.

167
00:05:11,621 --> 00:05:13,570
When you buy a television or a

168
00:05:13,570 --> 00:05:16,362
laptop, the checkout screen often offers an

169
00:05:16,362 --> 00:05:17,531
extended warranty.

170
00:05:17,711 --> 00:05:20,602
Stores push these hard because they're profitable.

171
00:05:20,782 --> 00:05:23,520
Consumer Reports has long argued that most

172
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:25,542
of them aren't worth the money,

173
00:05:25,542 --> 00:05:28,411
because many products don't break during the

174
00:05:28,411 --> 00:05:31,410
coverage period, and many credit cards already

175
00:05:31,410 --> 00:05:33,692
add some extra warranty protection.

176
00:05:34,042 --> 00:05:36,701
Wealthy people think about insurance differently.

177
00:05:36,881 --> 00:05:40,091
They insure against disasters they can't afford,

178
00:05:40,091 --> 00:05:42,164
like a house fire or a lawsuit.

179
00:05:42,344 --> 00:05:44,790
They don't insure against losses they could

180
00:05:44,790 --> 00:05:45,643
easily pay for,

181
00:05:45,643 --> 00:05:46,895
like a broken blender.

182
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:49,049
Instead, they self insure,

183
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:52,009
keeping cash ready for small surprises.

184
00:05:52,189 --> 00:05:54,537
Try this: next time you're offered a

185
00:05:54,537 --> 00:05:56,037
warranty, ask yourself,

186
00:05:56,037 --> 00:05:58,712
could I replace this item without hurting

187
00:05:58,712 --> 00:05:59,364
my budget?

188
00:05:59,544 --> 00:06:00,833
If yes, skip it.

189
00:06:01,182 --> 00:06:02,065
Number five.

190
00:06:02,245 --> 00:06:03,081
Timeshares.

191
00:06:03,430 --> 00:06:05,645
A timeshare lets you buy the right

192
00:06:05,645 --> 00:06:07,729
to use a vacation property for a

193
00:06:07,729 --> 00:06:09,096
week or so each year.

194
00:06:09,276 --> 00:06:11,504
The sales pitch is usually a free

195
00:06:11,504 --> 00:06:12,990
trip or a free dinner,

196
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:15,151
followed by a long presentation.

197
00:06:15,331 --> 00:06:17,244
The price can easily run into the

198
00:06:17,244 --> 00:06:18,925
tens of thousands of dollars,

199
00:06:18,925 --> 00:06:21,243
and there are yearly maintenance fees on

200
00:06:21,243 --> 00:06:23,098
top that tend to rise over time.

201
00:06:23,447 --> 00:06:25,467
The real problem shows up when you

202
00:06:25,467 --> 00:06:26,002
want out.

203
00:06:26,182 --> 00:06:28,763
The resale market for timeshares is weak,

204
00:06:28,763 --> 00:06:30,903
and many owners find they can sell

205
00:06:30,903 --> 00:06:32,981
only for a small fraction of what

206
00:06:32,981 --> 00:06:34,870
they paid, if they can sell at

207
00:06:34,870 --> 00:06:35,121
all.

208
00:06:35,301 --> 00:06:38,252
The Federal Trade Commission even warns about

209
00:06:38,252 --> 00:06:39,760
timeshare resale scams.

210
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:41,960
Rich people prefer flexibility.

211
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:43,920
They rent the vacation they want,

212
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,891
when they want it.

213
00:06:45,071 --> 00:06:46,893
Try this: if you're tempted,

214
00:06:46,893 --> 00:06:49,171
look up the resale listings for the

215
00:06:49,171 --> 00:06:50,342
same resort first.

216
00:06:50,692 --> 00:06:51,819
Number six.

217
00:06:51,999 --> 00:06:53,357
More house than they need.

218
00:06:53,706 --> 00:06:55,712
A bigger house doesn't just mean a

219
00:06:55,712 --> 00:06:56,655
bigger mortgage.

220
00:06:56,835 --> 00:06:59,126
It means bigger property taxes,

221
00:06:59,126 --> 00:07:02,894
bigger insurance, bigger heating and cooling bills,

222
00:07:02,894 --> 00:07:05,037
more furniture, more repairs,

223
00:07:05,037 --> 00:07:06,589
and more maintenance,

224
00:07:06,589 --> 00:07:08,805
every year, for as long as you

225
00:07:08,805 --> 00:07:09,618
live there.

226
00:07:09,798 --> 00:07:11,625
A common rule of thumb is to

227
00:07:11,625 --> 00:07:13,910
budget around one to two percent of

228
00:07:13,910 --> 00:07:16,063
a home's value each year just for

229
00:07:16,063 --> 00:07:16,520
upkeep.

230
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:19,980
Stanley's research found that many millionaires lived

231
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:21,975
in homes far below what they could

232
00:07:21,975 --> 00:07:22,385
afford.

233
00:07:22,565 --> 00:07:24,669
One of the most famous examples is

234
00:07:24,669 --> 00:07:27,020
Warren Buffett, who still lives in the

235
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:29,248
house in Omaha he bought in nineteen

236
00:07:29,248 --> 00:07:31,784
fifty eight for about thirty one thousand

237
00:07:31,784 --> 00:07:33,084
five hundred dollars.

238
00:07:33,264 --> 00:07:35,543
Try this: add up everything your home

239
00:07:35,543 --> 00:07:36,959
really costs per month,

240
00:07:36,959 --> 00:07:38,314
not just the mortgage.

241
00:07:38,494 --> 00:07:42,047
Taxes, insurance, utilities and repairs.

242
00:07:42,227 --> 00:07:44,224
That's your real housing cost.

243
00:07:44,573 --> 00:07:45,607
Number seven.

244
00:07:45,787 --> 00:07:46,890
Fees they can avoid.

245
00:07:47,239 --> 00:07:49,390
Overdraft fees, ATM fees,

246
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:51,626
late fees, high fund fees,

247
00:07:51,626 --> 00:07:53,776
account maintenance fees.

248
00:07:53,956 --> 00:07:55,140
Each one looks tiny.

249
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:56,946
But together they can add up to

250
00:07:56,946 --> 00:07:58,362
hundreds of dollars a year.

251
00:07:58,542 --> 00:08:00,957
And investment fees are the sneakiest,

252
00:08:00,957 --> 00:08:03,182
because a one percent yearly fee on

253
00:08:03,182 --> 00:08:05,343
a growing balance can cost tens of

254
00:08:05,343 --> 00:08:07,821
thousands of dollars over a lifetime of

255
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:08,457
investing.

256
00:08:08,806 --> 00:08:11,604
Rich people are almost allergic to fees.

257
00:08:11,784 --> 00:08:13,375
They read the fee schedule.

258
00:08:13,555 --> 00:08:15,339
They move their money to banks that

259
00:08:15,339 --> 00:08:16,562
don't charge for basics.

260
00:08:16,742 --> 00:08:18,623
They choose low cost funds.

261
00:08:18,803 --> 00:08:20,310
They pay bills on time,

262
00:08:20,310 --> 00:08:22,277
often with automatic payments,

263
00:08:22,277 --> 00:08:23,981
so late fees never happen.

264
00:08:24,161 --> 00:08:26,258
Try this: go through the last three

265
00:08:26,258 --> 00:08:28,774
months of bank statements and circle every

266
00:08:28,774 --> 00:08:29,014
fee.

267
00:08:29,194 --> 00:08:31,365
Then make one phone call or one

268
00:08:31,365 --> 00:08:33,536
switch to stop the biggest one.

269
00:08:33,885 --> 00:08:35,988
Before we move to the psychology,

270
00:08:35,988 --> 00:08:38,027
one more piece of math that ties

271
00:08:38,027 --> 00:08:39,238
all seven together.

272
00:08:39,418 --> 00:08:41,328
A dollar you don't spend is often

273
00:08:41,328 --> 00:08:43,295
worth more than a dollar you earn.

274
00:08:43,475 --> 00:08:44,311
Here's why.

275
00:08:44,491 --> 00:08:46,435
When you earn an extra dollar,

276
00:08:46,435 --> 00:08:48,056
taxes take a piece first.

277
00:08:48,236 --> 00:08:49,829
Federal income tax,

278
00:08:49,829 --> 00:08:52,260
Social Security and Medicare,

279
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:55,027
and in many places state tax too.

280
00:08:55,207 --> 00:08:56,842
Depending on your bracket,

281
00:08:56,842 --> 00:08:59,230
you might keep seventy or eighty cents

282
00:08:59,230 --> 00:08:59,608
of it.

283
00:08:59,957 --> 00:09:01,860
But when you avoid spending a dollar,

284
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:03,197
you keep the whole dollar.

285
00:09:03,377 --> 00:09:05,737
So cutting a one hundred dollar monthly

286
00:09:05,737 --> 00:09:07,856
fee or interest charge can be worth

287
00:09:07,856 --> 00:09:09,430
as much as a raise of well

288
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:11,791
over one hundred dollars a month before

289
00:09:11,791 --> 00:09:12,154
taxes.

290
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:15,347
That's why wealthy people take boring costs

291
00:09:15,347 --> 00:09:16,258
so seriously.

292
00:09:16,438 --> 00:09:18,043
Cutting a cost is like getting a

293
00:09:18,043 --> 00:09:19,648
raise that nobody can take away,

294
00:09:19,648 --> 00:09:21,153
and you don't have to ask your

295
00:09:21,153 --> 00:09:21,755
boss for it.

296
00:09:22,105 --> 00:09:23,336
And there's a second layer.

297
00:09:23,516 --> 00:09:25,659
Money you don't spend can be invested,

298
00:09:25,659 --> 00:09:27,463
and the growth on that money can

299
00:09:27,463 --> 00:09:28,648
compound for decades.

300
00:09:28,828 --> 00:09:31,119
So every one of these seven refusals

301
00:09:31,119 --> 00:09:32,392
has a triple effect.

302
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:33,698
You keep the full dollar.

303
00:09:33,878 --> 00:09:35,549
You avoid the loss that comes with

304
00:09:35,549 --> 00:09:36,189
the purchase.

305
00:09:36,369 --> 00:09:37,866
And you gain the future growth.

306
00:09:38,046 --> 00:09:40,259
Add those three together over a working

307
00:09:40,259 --> 00:09:42,584
lifetime, and you start to understand how

308
00:09:42,584 --> 00:09:45,024
ordinary earners become quiet millionaires.

309
00:09:45,373 --> 00:09:46,790
So that's angle one.

310
00:09:46,970 --> 00:09:47,643
The numbers.

311
00:09:47,823 --> 00:09:48,810
Notice something?

312
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:51,068
None of these are about being cheap.

313
00:09:51,248 --> 00:09:53,276
They're about not paying for things that

314
00:09:53,276 --> 00:09:55,254
make someone else rich at your expense.

315
00:09:55,434 --> 00:09:56,532
Now angle two,

316
00:09:56,532 --> 00:09:57,709
the psychology.

317
00:09:57,889 --> 00:10:00,681
Because the numbers alone don't explain why

318
00:10:00,681 --> 00:10:03,149
smart people keep buying these things.

319
00:10:03,498 --> 00:10:05,333
Here's the uncomfortable truth.

320
00:10:05,513 --> 00:10:07,879
Most people know a new car loses

321
00:10:07,879 --> 00:10:08,322
value.

322
00:10:08,502 --> 00:10:10,546
Most people know lottery odds are terrible.

323
00:10:10,726 --> 00:10:11,840
They buy anyway.

324
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:12,194
Why?

325
00:10:12,374 --> 00:10:15,010
Because spending isn't really a math decision.

326
00:10:15,190 --> 00:10:16,455
It's an emotional one.

327
00:10:16,635 --> 00:10:17,985
It's about status,

328
00:10:17,985 --> 00:10:20,084
comfort, belonging and fear.

329
00:10:20,264 --> 00:10:21,944
The next seven things on our list

330
00:10:21,944 --> 00:10:23,829
are the ones rich people skip because

331
00:10:23,829 --> 00:10:26,069
they've learned to see the psychology behind

332
00:10:26,069 --> 00:10:26,324
them.

333
00:10:26,673 --> 00:10:27,416
Number eight.

334
00:10:27,596 --> 00:10:28,885
Status symbols.

335
00:10:29,235 --> 00:10:31,755
Stanley described a type of person he

336
00:10:31,755 --> 00:10:34,274
called the income statement affluent.

337
00:10:34,454 --> 00:10:36,783
People with high incomes who look rich,

338
00:10:36,783 --> 00:10:37,918
with the big house,

339
00:10:37,918 --> 00:10:40,307
the luxury car and the designer clothes,

340
00:10:40,307 --> 00:10:42,337
but who have little actual wealth.

341
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:44,493
Their money goes out as fast as

342
00:10:44,493 --> 00:10:45,257
it comes in.

343
00:10:45,437 --> 00:10:47,873
He contrasted them with the balance sheet

344
00:10:47,873 --> 00:10:50,309
affluent, who might look ordinary but own

345
00:10:50,309 --> 00:10:51,022
real assets.

346
00:10:51,371 --> 00:10:53,753
The trap is that status purchases are

347
00:10:53,753 --> 00:10:55,040
designed to be seen.

348
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:56,478
And once you buy one,

349
00:10:56,478 --> 00:10:58,933
your new neighbors and coworkers have the

350
00:10:58,933 --> 00:11:01,089
same things, so the status fades and

351
00:11:01,089 --> 00:11:02,407
you need the next one.

352
00:11:02,587 --> 00:11:05,170
Economists call this keeping up with the

353
00:11:05,170 --> 00:11:05,687
Joneses.

354
00:11:05,867 --> 00:11:08,044
Try this: before a big purchase,

355
00:11:08,044 --> 00:11:10,086
ask, would I still buy this if

356
00:11:10,086 --> 00:11:11,788
nobody could ever see it?

357
00:11:11,968 --> 00:11:13,198
If the answer is no,

358
00:11:13,198 --> 00:11:15,044
you're paying for an audience.

359
00:11:15,394 --> 00:11:16,172
Number nine.

360
00:11:16,352 --> 00:11:18,434
Designer labels for the sake of the

361
00:11:18,434 --> 00:11:18,790
label.

362
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:21,517
Remember the numbers from The Millionaire Next

363
00:11:21,517 --> 00:11:21,776
Door.

364
00:11:21,956 --> 00:11:24,726
Most millionaires surveyed had never spent more

365
00:11:24,726 --> 00:11:27,143
than three hundred ninety nine dollars on

366
00:11:27,143 --> 00:11:29,265
a suit, one hundred forty dollars on

367
00:11:29,265 --> 00:11:31,682
shoes, or two hundred thirty five dollars

368
00:11:31,682 --> 00:11:32,625
on a wristwatch.

369
00:11:32,805 --> 00:11:35,247
Those were nineteen nineties dollars,

370
00:11:35,247 --> 00:11:36,830
so adjust for inflation,

371
00:11:36,830 --> 00:11:38,216
but the point stands.

372
00:11:38,396 --> 00:11:40,368
Most of them simply didn't care about

373
00:11:40,368 --> 00:11:40,741
labels.

374
00:11:41,090 --> 00:11:43,180
This doesn't mean rich people buy junk.

375
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,709
Many buy good quality items and use

376
00:11:45,709 --> 00:11:46,716
them for years.

377
00:11:46,896 --> 00:11:48,975
The difference is that they pay for

378
00:11:48,975 --> 00:11:50,402
quality, not for a logo.

379
00:11:50,582 --> 00:11:52,920
A plain, well made coat that lasts

380
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:55,602
ten years is cheaper than a fashionable

381
00:11:55,602 --> 00:11:56,909
one that lasts two.

382
00:11:57,089 --> 00:11:59,249
Try this: look at the five most

383
00:11:59,249 --> 00:12:01,478
expensive pieces in your closet.

384
00:12:01,658 --> 00:12:03,388
How often do you actually wear them?

385
00:12:03,568 --> 00:12:05,718
Divide the price by the number of

386
00:12:05,718 --> 00:12:06,435
times worn.

387
00:12:06,785 --> 00:12:07,575
Number ten.

388
00:12:07,755 --> 00:12:09,650
The newest phone the day it comes

389
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:09,880
out.

390
00:12:10,229 --> 00:12:13,313
Flagship phones can cost a thousand dollars

391
00:12:13,313 --> 00:12:13,887
or more.

392
00:12:14,067 --> 00:12:15,458
Upgrading every year,

393
00:12:15,458 --> 00:12:17,909
often through a monthly payment plan,

394
00:12:17,909 --> 00:12:19,697
makes that cost feel small.

395
00:12:19,877 --> 00:12:22,324
But each new model usually brings modest

396
00:12:22,324 --> 00:12:24,220
improvements over the last one.

397
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:26,705
Many people pay full price for features

398
00:12:26,705 --> 00:12:27,592
they never use.

399
00:12:27,942 --> 00:12:30,392
The psychology here is novelty.

400
00:12:30,572 --> 00:12:32,782
Your brain gets a little reward from

401
00:12:32,782 --> 00:12:34,255
something new and shiny,

402
00:12:34,255 --> 00:12:37,324
and companies schedule launches to trigger exactly

403
00:12:37,324 --> 00:12:37,631
that.

404
00:12:37,811 --> 00:12:40,258
Many wealthy people keep their devices until

405
00:12:40,258 --> 00:12:42,037
they actually stop working well.

406
00:12:42,217 --> 00:12:44,351
Try this: when the next launch comes,

407
00:12:44,351 --> 00:12:46,945
write down three specific things your current

408
00:12:46,945 --> 00:12:49,137
phone can't do that you actually need.

409
00:12:49,317 --> 00:12:50,934
If you can't find three,

410
00:12:50,934 --> 00:12:51,743
wait a year.

411
00:12:52,093 --> 00:12:53,022
Number eleven.

412
00:12:53,202 --> 00:12:56,058
Things bought to impress strangers online.

413
00:12:56,408 --> 00:12:58,932
Social media turned everyone's life into a

414
00:12:58,932 --> 00:12:59,833
highlight reel.

415
00:13:00,013 --> 00:13:01,660
You see the vacation,

416
00:13:01,660 --> 00:13:02,915
the new kitchen,

417
00:13:02,915 --> 00:13:03,543
the car.

418
00:13:03,723 --> 00:13:05,430
You don't see the credit card bill.

419
00:13:05,610 --> 00:13:08,436
Surveys from groups like Bankrate and Credit

420
00:13:08,436 --> 00:13:11,005
Karma have found that a meaningful share

421
00:13:11,005 --> 00:13:14,024
of younger adults say they've overspent because

422
00:13:14,024 --> 00:13:16,465
of something they saw on social media.

423
00:13:16,814 --> 00:13:19,567
Rich people are often remarkably private about

424
00:13:19,567 --> 00:13:19,926
money.

425
00:13:20,106 --> 00:13:24,574
Stanley's millionaires valued financial independence over social

426
00:13:24,574 --> 00:13:25,133
display.

427
00:13:25,313 --> 00:13:27,524
If you're not trying to impress anyone,

428
00:13:27,524 --> 00:13:29,339
you're free to spend on what you

429
00:13:29,339 --> 00:13:30,189
actually enjoy.

430
00:13:30,369 --> 00:13:33,032
Try this: unfollow five accounts that make

431
00:13:33,032 --> 00:13:34,491
you want to buy things.

432
00:13:34,671 --> 00:13:36,570
Notice how your wants change in the

433
00:13:36,570 --> 00:13:37,167
next month.

434
00:13:37,516 --> 00:13:38,596
Quick pause.

435
00:13:38,776 --> 00:13:40,823
If you're getting value from this,

436
00:13:40,823 --> 00:13:42,629
do me a favor and subscribe to

437
00:13:42,629 --> 00:13:43,652
Ryan Rover Money.

438
00:13:43,832 --> 00:13:45,934
It's free, it helps the channel a

439
00:13:45,934 --> 00:13:48,036
lot, and every week we break down

440
00:13:48,036 --> 00:13:49,501
how money really works,

441
00:13:49,501 --> 00:13:50,647
with real numbers.

442
00:13:50,827 --> 00:13:53,033
Okay, back to the psychology.

443
00:13:53,213 --> 00:13:55,342
Number twelve is one that hits close

444
00:13:55,342 --> 00:13:56,998
to home for a lot of people.

445
00:13:57,348 --> 00:13:58,242
Number twelve.

446
00:13:58,422 --> 00:14:00,350
Convenience at any price.

447
00:14:00,699 --> 00:14:02,484
Delivery fees, service fees,

448
00:14:02,484 --> 00:14:04,715
tips, and marked up menu prices can

449
00:14:04,715 --> 00:14:07,009
make a delivered meal cost much more

450
00:14:07,009 --> 00:14:08,985
than the same food picked up or

451
00:14:08,985 --> 00:14:09,941
cooked at home.

452
00:14:10,121 --> 00:14:11,584
Doing it once is fine.

453
00:14:11,764 --> 00:14:14,051
Doing it every day quietly becomes one

454
00:14:14,051 --> 00:14:16,037
of the biggest lines in a budget,

455
00:14:16,037 --> 00:14:17,964
and most people never add it up.

456
00:14:18,314 --> 00:14:20,576
Wealthy people do pay for convenience,

457
00:14:20,576 --> 00:14:21,588
but deliberately.

458
00:14:21,768 --> 00:14:24,004
They might pay for help with something

459
00:14:24,004 --> 00:14:25,299
that saves them hours,

460
00:14:25,299 --> 00:14:26,653
like a big home repair,

461
00:14:26,653 --> 00:14:29,302
while still cooking simple meals most nights.

462
00:14:29,482 --> 00:14:30,794
The rule is intention.

463
00:14:30,974 --> 00:14:33,785
Try this: check your delivery app history

464
00:14:33,785 --> 00:14:35,773
for the last month and add up

465
00:14:35,773 --> 00:14:38,173
the total, including fees and tips.

466
00:14:38,353 --> 00:14:40,324
Then decide what that number should be

467
00:14:40,324 --> 00:14:40,895
next month.

468
00:14:41,246 --> 00:14:42,395
Number thirteen.

469
00:14:42,575 --> 00:14:44,003
Retail therapy.

470
00:14:44,353 --> 00:14:46,107
Buying something can lift your mood for

471
00:14:46,107 --> 00:14:46,512
a moment.

472
00:14:46,692 --> 00:14:48,515
Researchers have studied this,

473
00:14:48,515 --> 00:14:51,129
and shopping can genuinely make people feel

474
00:14:51,129 --> 00:14:52,648
better in the short term.

475
00:14:52,828 --> 00:14:54,899
The problem is that the feeling fades

476
00:14:54,899 --> 00:14:56,578
quickly, and the bill doesn't.

477
00:14:56,758 --> 00:14:59,521
Over time, using shopping to manage stress

478
00:14:59,521 --> 00:15:01,495
can become an expensive habit.

479
00:15:01,845 --> 00:15:04,348
Many rich people have replaced spending with

480
00:15:04,348 --> 00:15:05,827
cheaper ways to feel good.

481
00:15:06,007 --> 00:15:08,375
Walking, exercise, reading,

482
00:15:08,375 --> 00:15:09,954
time with friends.

483
00:15:10,134 --> 00:15:11,713
Not because they're saints,

484
00:15:11,713 --> 00:15:14,462
but because they noticed the difference between

485
00:15:14,462 --> 00:15:16,450
a purchase that adds value and one

486
00:15:16,450 --> 00:15:18,029
that just covers a bad day.

487
00:15:18,209 --> 00:15:20,749
Try this: use a twenty four hour

488
00:15:20,749 --> 00:15:21,146
rule.

489
00:15:21,326 --> 00:15:23,836
If you want something that isn't essential,

490
00:15:23,836 --> 00:15:25,411
put it in the cart and wait

491
00:15:25,411 --> 00:15:25,761
a day.

492
00:15:25,941 --> 00:15:27,880
Often, the urge passes.

493
00:15:28,231 --> 00:15:29,345
Number fourteen.

494
00:15:29,525 --> 00:15:31,905
Financial products they don't understand.

495
00:15:32,256 --> 00:15:35,563
Some investment products are sold with complicated

496
00:15:35,563 --> 00:15:37,944
features, guarantees and fine print.

497
00:15:38,124 --> 00:15:40,103
Some of them come with high fees

498
00:15:40,103 --> 00:15:41,649
and long lock up periods,

499
00:15:41,649 --> 00:15:43,628
with penalties to get out early.

500
00:15:43,808 --> 00:15:46,458
The complexity isn't always there to help

501
00:15:46,458 --> 00:15:47,105
the buyer.

502
00:15:47,285 --> 00:15:49,467
Sometimes it's there to hide the cost.

503
00:15:49,818 --> 00:15:52,212
Warren Buffett has famously said he only

504
00:15:52,212 --> 00:15:54,427
invests in businesses he understands.

505
00:15:54,607 --> 00:15:57,139
Many wealthy people follow a version of

506
00:15:57,139 --> 00:15:57,788
that rule.

507
00:15:57,968 --> 00:15:59,897
If they can't explain how a product

508
00:15:59,897 --> 00:16:01,550
makes money and what it costs,

509
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:02,542
they don't buy it.

510
00:16:02,722 --> 00:16:04,918
Try this: if someone pitches you an

511
00:16:04,918 --> 00:16:06,925
investment, ask three questions.

512
00:16:07,105 --> 00:16:08,138
How do you get paid?

513
00:16:08,318 --> 00:16:09,723
What are all the fees?

514
00:16:09,903 --> 00:16:11,610
How do I get my money out?

515
00:16:11,790 --> 00:16:13,415
If the answers are vague,

516
00:16:13,415 --> 00:16:14,065
walk away.

517
00:16:14,416 --> 00:16:15,402
That's angle two.

518
00:16:15,582 --> 00:16:18,138
Status, novelty, convenience,

519
00:16:18,138 --> 00:16:19,901
emotion, complexity.

520
00:16:20,081 --> 00:16:22,540
Rich people feel all of those pulls

521
00:16:22,540 --> 00:16:22,821
too.

522
00:16:23,001 --> 00:16:24,650
They've just learned to notice them.

523
00:16:24,830 --> 00:16:26,386
Now the best part.

524
00:16:26,566 --> 00:16:27,425
Angle three.

525
00:16:27,605 --> 00:16:28,429
The playbook.

526
00:16:28,609 --> 00:16:30,606
What do rich people buy instead,

527
00:16:30,606 --> 00:16:32,104
and how can you copy it?

528
00:16:32,454 --> 00:16:33,417
Here's the shift.

529
00:16:33,597 --> 00:16:35,373
Rich people don't just avoid things.

530
00:16:35,553 --> 00:16:37,743
They redirect the money toward things that

531
00:16:37,743 --> 00:16:38,003
grow.

532
00:16:38,183 --> 00:16:40,170
Every item in this last group is

533
00:16:40,170 --> 00:16:41,970
something they refuse to buy,

534
00:16:41,970 --> 00:16:44,081
paired with what they buy instead.

535
00:16:44,261 --> 00:16:45,898
That pairing is the whole playbook.

536
00:16:46,248 --> 00:16:47,339
Number fifteen.

537
00:16:47,519 --> 00:16:50,317
Lifestyle upgrades before they own assets.

538
00:16:50,667 --> 00:16:53,267
A common pattern among people who build

539
00:16:53,267 --> 00:16:55,868
wealth is a simple order of operations.

540
00:16:56,048 --> 00:16:59,084
First, they build income producing assets,

541
00:16:59,084 --> 00:17:00,892
like retirement accounts,

542
00:17:00,892 --> 00:17:04,001
index funds, a business or rental property.

543
00:17:04,181 --> 00:17:06,669
Then, once those assets are growing,

544
00:17:06,669 --> 00:17:08,812
they use part of the growth for

545
00:17:08,812 --> 00:17:10,125
lifestyle upgrades.

546
00:17:10,305 --> 00:17:11,925
Most people do it the other way

547
00:17:11,925 --> 00:17:12,290
around.

548
00:17:12,641 --> 00:17:13,802
Think about a raise.

549
00:17:13,982 --> 00:17:16,485
Most people upgrade their lifestyle the same

550
00:17:16,485 --> 00:17:16,826
month.

551
00:17:17,006 --> 00:17:19,627
Many wealthy people save the first raise,

552
00:17:19,627 --> 00:17:21,097
or at least half of it,

553
00:17:21,097 --> 00:17:22,950
before anything else changes.

554
00:17:23,130 --> 00:17:25,187
That way their spending never catches up

555
00:17:25,187 --> 00:17:26,010
to their income.

556
00:17:26,190 --> 00:17:28,049
Try this: next time you get a

557
00:17:28,049 --> 00:17:30,485
raise, set up an automatic transfer of

558
00:17:30,485 --> 00:17:33,433
half the increase into investments before your

559
00:17:33,433 --> 00:17:35,164
first new paycheck arrives.

560
00:17:35,514 --> 00:17:36,593
Number sixteen.

561
00:17:36,773 --> 00:17:38,599
Cheap tools that break,

562
00:17:38,599 --> 00:17:39,711
over and over.

563
00:17:40,061 --> 00:17:41,860
This one surprises people.

564
00:17:42,040 --> 00:17:44,046
Rich people often refuse to buy the

565
00:17:44,046 --> 00:17:46,397
cheapest version of things they use every

566
00:17:46,397 --> 00:17:46,626
day.

567
00:17:46,806 --> 00:17:48,799
A very cheap pair of boots that

568
00:17:48,799 --> 00:17:51,113
falls apart every year can cost more

569
00:17:51,113 --> 00:17:53,105
over ten years than a well made

570
00:17:53,105 --> 00:17:54,712
pair that lasts a decade.

571
00:17:54,892 --> 00:17:57,360
The writer Terry Pratchett called this the

572
00:17:57,360 --> 00:17:58,770
boots theory of poverty.

573
00:17:59,121 --> 00:18:00,932
The key is the word every day.

574
00:18:01,112 --> 00:18:03,295
For things you use constantly,

575
00:18:03,295 --> 00:18:04,460
like a mattress,

576
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:06,934
work shoes, a reliable car or good

577
00:18:06,934 --> 00:18:09,773
kitchen knives, quality can save money.

578
00:18:09,953 --> 00:18:11,984
For things you use once a year,

579
00:18:11,984 --> 00:18:12,902
cheap is fine.

580
00:18:13,082 --> 00:18:15,496
Try this: list the five things you

581
00:18:15,496 --> 00:18:16,135
use most.

582
00:18:16,315 --> 00:18:18,486
Are any of them cheap replacements you

583
00:18:18,486 --> 00:18:19,171
keep buying?

584
00:18:19,351 --> 00:18:20,826
That's where quality pays.

585
00:18:21,176 --> 00:18:22,302
Number seventeen.

586
00:18:22,482 --> 00:18:24,444
Complex insurance they don't need.

587
00:18:24,794 --> 00:18:27,114
Life insurance matters if people depend on

588
00:18:27,114 --> 00:18:27,777
your income.

589
00:18:27,957 --> 00:18:30,813
But some policies mix insurance with investing,

590
00:18:30,813 --> 00:18:32,636
and they can carry high costs.

591
00:18:32,816 --> 00:18:36,185
Many personal finance experts suggest that most

592
00:18:36,185 --> 00:18:38,981
families are well served by simple term

593
00:18:38,981 --> 00:18:41,919
life insurance, which covers a set number

594
00:18:41,919 --> 00:18:44,356
of years for a much lower premium.

595
00:18:44,707 --> 00:18:47,167
The principle is to keep insurance and

596
00:18:47,167 --> 00:18:49,691
investing separate, so you can see what

597
00:18:49,691 --> 00:18:50,662
each one costs.

598
00:18:50,842 --> 00:18:52,431
Buy the coverage you need,

599
00:18:52,431 --> 00:18:55,118
compare quotes, and invest the difference in

600
00:18:55,118 --> 00:18:56,218
low cost accounts.

601
00:18:56,398 --> 00:18:59,124
Some people do have reasons for permanent

602
00:18:59,124 --> 00:19:01,384
policies, so this is a question to

603
00:19:01,384 --> 00:19:03,312
ask a fee only advisor about,

604
00:19:03,312 --> 00:19:04,908
not a rule for everyone.

605
00:19:05,088 --> 00:19:07,441
Try this: find your current policy and

606
00:19:07,441 --> 00:19:09,484
write down what you pay each year

607
00:19:09,484 --> 00:19:10,661
and what it covers.

608
00:19:11,011 --> 00:19:12,114
Number eighteen.

609
00:19:12,294 --> 00:19:13,768
Brand name basics.

610
00:19:14,118 --> 00:19:15,570
Store brand groceries,

611
00:19:15,570 --> 00:19:18,674
medicines and household products are often made

612
00:19:18,674 --> 00:19:21,316
to similar standards as the name brands,

613
00:19:21,316 --> 00:19:23,231
and can cost noticeably less.

614
00:19:23,411 --> 00:19:25,245
For over the counter medicines,

615
00:19:25,245 --> 00:19:27,492
the active ingredient is listed on the

616
00:19:27,492 --> 00:19:29,739
label, and the store version often has

617
00:19:29,739 --> 00:19:30,981
exactly the same one.

618
00:19:31,161 --> 00:19:34,586
The difference is mostly packaging and advertising.

619
00:19:34,936 --> 00:19:37,698
Researchers in Chicago once studied shoppers and

620
00:19:37,698 --> 00:19:40,229
found that pharmacists and doctors were much

621
00:19:40,229 --> 00:19:42,474
more likely to buy store brand headache

622
00:19:42,474 --> 00:19:44,142
medicine than other shoppers.

623
00:19:44,322 --> 00:19:46,470
When people know what's inside the box,

624
00:19:46,470 --> 00:19:48,177
they stop paying for the brand.

625
00:19:48,357 --> 00:19:51,161
Try this: on your next grocery trip,

626
00:19:51,161 --> 00:19:53,965
swap five name brand items for store

627
00:19:53,965 --> 00:19:54,510
brands.

628
00:19:54,690 --> 00:19:56,540
If you can't tell the difference,

629
00:19:56,540 --> 00:19:57,326
keep the swap.

630
00:19:57,676 --> 00:19:58,697
Number nineteen.

631
00:19:58,877 --> 00:20:00,688
Subscriptions they don't use.

632
00:20:01,039 --> 00:20:02,933
Streaming services, apps,

633
00:20:02,933 --> 00:20:06,344
gym memberships, boxes delivered every month.

634
00:20:06,524 --> 00:20:08,405
Each one costs a few dollars,

635
00:20:08,405 --> 00:20:10,286
and many renew automatically.

636
00:20:10,466 --> 00:20:13,754
Surveys regularly find that people underestimate how

637
00:20:13,754 --> 00:20:15,841
much they spend on subscriptions,

638
00:20:15,841 --> 00:20:17,548
sometimes by a wide margin.

639
00:20:17,728 --> 00:20:19,957
The business model depends on you forgetting.

640
00:20:20,308 --> 00:20:23,234
Rich people treat recurring costs as serious,

641
00:20:23,234 --> 00:20:25,446
because a recurring cost is like a

642
00:20:25,446 --> 00:20:26,681
reverse investment.

643
00:20:26,861 --> 00:20:28,893
Fifteen dollars a month for ten years

644
00:20:28,893 --> 00:20:30,431
is eighteen hundred dollars,

645
00:20:30,431 --> 00:20:32,518
before you count what that money could

646
00:20:32,518 --> 00:20:33,177
have earned.

647
00:20:33,357 --> 00:20:35,549
Try this: search your bank and card

648
00:20:35,549 --> 00:20:38,117
statements for every charge that repeats.

649
00:20:38,297 --> 00:20:40,432
Cancel anything you haven't used in the

650
00:20:40,432 --> 00:20:41,362
last thirty days.

651
00:20:41,542 --> 00:20:43,075
You can always sign up again.

652
00:20:43,425 --> 00:20:44,574
And number twenty.

653
00:20:44,754 --> 00:20:46,333
Deals that waste their time.

654
00:20:46,683 --> 00:20:48,180
This is the twist ending.

655
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:50,795
Rich people don't chase every coupon or

656
00:20:50,795 --> 00:20:53,167
drive across town to save two dollars.

657
00:20:53,347 --> 00:20:55,373
Time is the one thing nobody can

658
00:20:55,373 --> 00:20:56,133
buy more of.

659
00:20:56,313 --> 00:20:58,026
A deal that costs an hour to

660
00:20:58,026 --> 00:20:59,800
save a few dollars is often a

661
00:20:59,800 --> 00:21:02,308
bad trade, and buying things just because

662
00:21:02,308 --> 00:21:04,510
they're on sale isn't saving at all.

663
00:21:04,690 --> 00:21:05,491
It's spending.

664
00:21:05,841 --> 00:21:08,148
The wealthy focus on the big decisions,

665
00:21:08,148 --> 00:21:09,567
where the real money is.

666
00:21:09,747 --> 00:21:11,942
Housing, cars, taxes,

667
00:21:11,942 --> 00:21:13,927
fees and investing.

668
00:21:14,107 --> 00:21:15,315
Get those five right,

669
00:21:15,315 --> 00:21:17,212
and you can relax about the price

670
00:21:17,212 --> 00:21:17,787
of coffee.

671
00:21:17,967 --> 00:21:18,875
Get them wrong,

672
00:21:18,875 --> 00:21:21,115
and no amount of coupon clipping will

673
00:21:21,115 --> 00:21:21,659
save you.

674
00:21:21,839 --> 00:21:24,613
Try this: rank your five biggest yearly

675
00:21:24,613 --> 00:21:25,253
expenses.

676
00:21:25,433 --> 00:21:27,810
Spend your energy cutting the top one,

677
00:21:27,810 --> 00:21:29,125
not the smallest one.

678
00:21:29,475 --> 00:21:31,851
Before we wrap up the three angles,

679
00:21:31,851 --> 00:21:33,480
let me tell you a story.

680
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:34,437
It's made up,

681
00:21:34,437 --> 00:21:36,827
but it's built from the patterns Stanley

682
00:21:36,827 --> 00:21:38,141
found again and again.

683
00:21:38,492 --> 00:21:40,895
Meet two neighbors on the same street.

684
00:21:41,075 --> 00:21:43,245
Mark is a sales director earning two

685
00:21:43,245 --> 00:21:45,173
hundred thousand dollars a year.

686
00:21:45,353 --> 00:21:47,537
Linda is a high school teacher married

687
00:21:47,537 --> 00:21:48,571
to an electrician,

688
00:21:48,571 --> 00:21:50,870
and together they earn about one hundred

689
00:21:50,870 --> 00:21:52,249
thirty thousand dollars.

690
00:21:52,429 --> 00:21:53,350
From the street,

691
00:21:53,350 --> 00:21:55,019
Mark looks like the rich one.

692
00:21:55,199 --> 00:21:56,825
He has the bigger house,

693
00:21:56,825 --> 00:21:59,197
a new luxury SUV every three years,

694
00:21:59,197 --> 00:22:01,027
and a boat in the driveway.

695
00:22:01,377 --> 00:22:03,472
Linda and her husband drive a ten

696
00:22:03,472 --> 00:22:05,695
year old minivan and a pickup truck

697
00:22:05,695 --> 00:22:06,775
they bought used.

698
00:22:06,955 --> 00:22:09,310
Their house is smaller and paid down

699
00:22:09,310 --> 00:22:09,637
fast.

700
00:22:09,817 --> 00:22:11,166
They cook most meals,

701
00:22:11,166 --> 00:22:13,220
take one simple vacation a year,

702
00:22:13,220 --> 00:22:15,532
and since their first jobs they have

703
00:22:15,532 --> 00:22:17,908
put fifteen percent of their pay into

704
00:22:17,908 --> 00:22:20,219
retirement accounts and index funds,

705
00:22:20,219 --> 00:22:22,402
automatically, every single month.

706
00:22:22,753 --> 00:22:24,632
Twenty five years later,

707
00:22:24,632 --> 00:22:26,356
Mark has a big income,

708
00:22:26,356 --> 00:22:28,549
a big mortgage and a big car

709
00:22:28,549 --> 00:22:28,941
loan.

710
00:22:29,121 --> 00:22:30,385
If he lost his job,

711
00:22:30,385 --> 00:22:33,115
his family could last maybe three months.

712
00:22:33,295 --> 00:22:35,413
Linda and her husband have a paid

713
00:22:35,413 --> 00:22:37,402
off house and investments that,

714
00:22:37,402 --> 00:22:39,328
at historical average returns,

715
00:22:39,328 --> 00:22:41,767
could plausibly be well over a million

716
00:22:41,767 --> 00:22:42,281
dollars.

717
00:22:42,461 --> 00:22:44,225
They could stop working tomorrow.

718
00:22:44,405 --> 00:22:46,774
Mark can't stop for a single month.

719
00:22:47,124 --> 00:22:50,560
Stanley's research suggested this pattern was common.

720
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:51,645
He found that,

721
00:22:51,645 --> 00:22:53,520
among high income households,

722
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:56,105
the ones who accumulated the most wealth

723
00:22:56,105 --> 00:22:58,368
were usually the ones who spent the

724
00:22:58,368 --> 00:23:00,307
least on visible status items.

725
00:23:00,487 --> 00:23:02,008
Income makes you look rich.

726
00:23:02,188 --> 00:23:03,604
Wealth makes you free.

727
00:23:03,784 --> 00:23:05,461
And the twenty things on this list

728
00:23:05,461 --> 00:23:07,778
are exactly where that difference gets decided.

729
00:23:08,128 --> 00:23:10,432
Now let's put the three angles together,

730
00:23:10,432 --> 00:23:12,679
because this is where it gets exciting.

731
00:23:13,029 --> 00:23:15,462
Imagine someone who skips just a few

732
00:23:15,462 --> 00:23:16,070
of these.

733
00:23:16,250 --> 00:23:18,276
They keep their car a few years

734
00:23:18,276 --> 00:23:20,105
longer instead of upgrading.

735
00:23:20,285 --> 00:23:22,398
They pay their credit card in full.

736
00:23:22,578 --> 00:23:25,140
They cancel two subscriptions they don't use

737
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:26,595
and cut back on delivery.

738
00:23:26,775 --> 00:23:28,974
It's not hard to imagine that freeing

739
00:23:28,974 --> 00:23:31,055
up five hundred dollars a month for

740
00:23:31,055 --> 00:23:32,243
a typical household.

741
00:23:32,423 --> 00:23:33,921
Not by living in misery.

742
00:23:34,101 --> 00:23:36,080
Just by refusing to pay for things

743
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:38,060
that don't add much to their life.

744
00:23:38,410 --> 00:23:40,763
Now invest that five hundred dollars a

745
00:23:40,763 --> 00:23:43,177
month at a seven percent average yearly

746
00:23:43,177 --> 00:23:43,611
return.

747
00:23:43,791 --> 00:23:44,976
After thirty years,

748
00:23:44,976 --> 00:23:47,346
that could grow to roughly six hundred

749
00:23:47,346 --> 00:23:48,655
ten thousand dollars.

750
00:23:48,835 --> 00:23:50,595
You would have put in one hundred

751
00:23:50,595 --> 00:23:51,448
eighty thousand.

752
00:23:51,628 --> 00:23:52,707
The rest is growth.

753
00:23:53,058 --> 00:23:55,244
That's the secret Stanley kept finding in

754
00:23:55,244 --> 00:23:55,937
his research.

755
00:23:56,117 --> 00:23:58,461
The millionaire next door didn't get there

756
00:23:58,461 --> 00:23:59,577
through one big win.

757
00:23:59,757 --> 00:24:02,257
They got there by quietly refusing hundreds

758
00:24:02,257 --> 00:24:04,002
of small and medium purchases,

759
00:24:04,002 --> 00:24:05,979
and putting that money to work for

760
00:24:05,979 --> 00:24:06,444
decades.

761
00:24:06,624 --> 00:24:07,669
It's not exciting.

762
00:24:07,849 --> 00:24:09,184
It's not glamorous.

763
00:24:09,364 --> 00:24:11,473
But it works for ordinary people with

764
00:24:11,473 --> 00:24:13,867
ordinary incomes, and that's what makes it

765
00:24:13,867 --> 00:24:14,380
powerful.

766
00:24:14,730 --> 00:24:16,920
So if rich people refuse all these

767
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:19,432
things, what do they actually spend on?

768
00:24:19,612 --> 00:24:21,051
Because they do spend.

769
00:24:21,231 --> 00:24:22,590
They just spend differently.

770
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:24,565
First, they buy time.

771
00:24:24,745 --> 00:24:27,341
Many wealthy people will happily pay for

772
00:24:27,341 --> 00:24:29,028
things that free up hours,

773
00:24:29,028 --> 00:24:30,909
like help with a big project,

774
00:24:30,909 --> 00:24:32,986
or living close to work to avoid

775
00:24:32,986 --> 00:24:34,219
a two hour commute.

776
00:24:34,399 --> 00:24:36,614
Time saved can go into a business,

777
00:24:36,614 --> 00:24:39,089
a skill, their health or their family.

778
00:24:39,269 --> 00:24:41,057
That's spending that pays them back.

779
00:24:41,407 --> 00:24:43,322
Second, they buy knowledge.

780
00:24:43,502 --> 00:24:45,523
Books, courses, coaching,

781
00:24:45,523 --> 00:24:47,705
conferences in their field.

782
00:24:47,885 --> 00:24:50,305
Stanley noticed that many of the millionaires

783
00:24:50,305 --> 00:24:52,563
he studied were serious about learning and

784
00:24:52,563 --> 00:24:55,037
about investing in their children's education.

785
00:24:55,217 --> 00:24:57,068
A skill that raises your income for

786
00:24:57,068 --> 00:24:58,655
the rest of your career can be

787
00:24:58,655 --> 00:25:00,348
the best return you'll ever get.

788
00:25:00,699 --> 00:25:02,150
Third, they buy health.

789
00:25:02,330 --> 00:25:04,220
Good food, exercise,

790
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:06,393
preventive care, sleep.

791
00:25:06,573 --> 00:25:09,045
Health problems are one of the biggest

792
00:25:09,045 --> 00:25:11,062
financial risks a family faces,

793
00:25:11,062 --> 00:25:13,534
and medical bills are a leading reason

794
00:25:13,534 --> 00:25:14,770
people fall behind.

795
00:25:14,950 --> 00:25:17,362
Spending a little on staying healthy is

796
00:25:17,362 --> 00:25:19,218
protecting your biggest asset,

797
00:25:19,218 --> 00:25:21,259
which is your ability to work and

798
00:25:21,259 --> 00:25:21,939
enjoy life.

799
00:25:22,290 --> 00:25:24,124
Fourth, they buy assets.

800
00:25:24,304 --> 00:25:26,598
Index funds, retirement accounts,

801
00:25:26,598 --> 00:25:28,127
a share of a business,

802
00:25:28,127 --> 00:25:29,378
a rental property.

803
00:25:29,558 --> 00:25:31,799
Things that tend to grow or pay

804
00:25:31,799 --> 00:25:33,029
income over time.

805
00:25:33,209 --> 00:25:35,808
And fifth, they buy experiences with the

806
00:25:35,808 --> 00:25:36,913
people they love.

807
00:25:37,093 --> 00:25:40,174
Research on happiness has often suggested that

808
00:25:40,174 --> 00:25:42,586
experiences, especially shared ones,

809
00:25:42,586 --> 00:25:45,533
bring more lasting satisfaction than things.

810
00:25:45,713 --> 00:25:47,831
A camping trip with your kids can

811
00:25:47,831 --> 00:25:49,821
stay with you longer than a new

812
00:25:49,821 --> 00:25:50,206
couch.

813
00:25:50,556 --> 00:25:51,531
Notice the pattern.

814
00:25:51,711 --> 00:25:53,821
Time, knowledge, health,

815
00:25:53,821 --> 00:25:56,018
assets and relationships.

816
00:25:56,198 --> 00:25:57,984
Every one of them either grows in

817
00:25:57,984 --> 00:25:59,879
value or makes your life better for

818
00:25:59,879 --> 00:26:00,204
years.

819
00:26:00,384 --> 00:26:02,496
Compare that with the twenty things on

820
00:26:02,496 --> 00:26:04,553
our list, which mostly lose value the

821
00:26:04,553 --> 00:26:05,387
moment you pay.

822
00:26:05,567 --> 00:26:08,185
That's the real difference between how rich

823
00:26:08,185 --> 00:26:10,072
people and everyone else spend.

824
00:26:10,422 --> 00:26:11,838
Now, a challenge.

825
00:26:12,018 --> 00:26:14,248
Let's make this practical with a thirty

826
00:26:14,248 --> 00:26:15,106
day experiment.

827
00:26:15,457 --> 00:26:17,047
Week one, the numbers.

828
00:26:17,227 --> 00:26:19,003
Pull up your last three months of

829
00:26:19,003 --> 00:26:19,596
statements.

830
00:26:19,776 --> 00:26:20,952
Highlight every fee,

831
00:26:20,952 --> 00:26:22,246
every interest charge,

832
00:26:22,246 --> 00:26:24,657
and every payment on something that loses

833
00:26:24,657 --> 00:26:26,010
value, like a car loan.

834
00:26:26,190 --> 00:26:27,490
Don't change anything yet.

835
00:26:27,670 --> 00:26:28,831
Just see the total.

836
00:26:29,011 --> 00:26:31,185
Most people are surprised by it,

837
00:26:31,185 --> 00:26:33,087
and that surprise is useful.

838
00:26:33,437 --> 00:26:35,457
Week two, the psychology.

839
00:26:35,637 --> 00:26:37,597
Every time you want to buy something

840
00:26:37,597 --> 00:26:38,740
that isn't essential,

841
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:40,374
write it down with the reason.

842
00:26:40,554 --> 00:26:41,355
Was it boredom?

843
00:26:41,535 --> 00:26:42,104
Stress?

844
00:26:42,284 --> 00:26:43,630
Something you saw online?

845
00:26:43,810 --> 00:26:45,087
Something a friend has?

846
00:26:45,267 --> 00:26:46,452
Don't judge yourself.

847
00:26:46,632 --> 00:26:47,468
Just name it.

848
00:26:47,648 --> 00:26:48,947
By the end of the week,

849
00:26:48,947 --> 00:26:51,038
you'll see your own patterns clearly.

850
00:26:51,388 --> 00:26:53,071
Week three, the playbook.

851
00:26:53,251 --> 00:26:54,540
Make three changes.

852
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:56,960
Cancel the subscriptions you don't use.

853
00:26:57,140 --> 00:26:59,300
Call to remove one fee or lower

854
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:00,554
one interest rate.

855
00:27:00,734 --> 00:27:03,283
And set up one automatic transfer into

856
00:27:03,283 --> 00:27:05,497
savings or investments on payday,

857
00:27:05,497 --> 00:27:06,771
even if it's small.

